<Template>
  <!-- One stem of one deck. `channel` comes from waveform.xml, `stem` from
       stems.xml, and the two group names built here are Mixxx 2.6's own:

           [ChannelN_StemM]                    volume, mute, color
           [QuickEffectRack1_[ChannelN_StemM]] the stem's own QuickEffect

       which is the shape the stock 2.6 skins build in their stem_channel.xml.
       Three of those are what the DDJ-1000 has a hand on, the channel fader,
       the CUE button and the COLOR knob, so those are the three this row
       draws, in that order after the name. The chain *preset* is deliberately
       not here: a combo box on a row this tall cannot be hit with a finger, and
       the unit has no per-stem control that would select one either. Choosing
       which effect the COLOR knob runs on a stem is not reachable from this
       skin yet, and that is a gap rather than an oversight.

       The name is the file's, not ours. WStemLabel reads the manifest of
       whatever is loaded and writes that stem's own label into the text; all
       the skin picks is StemNum. It writes the manifest's colour into the
       palette as well, and that part does not survive this skin's stylesheet;
       style.qss says what was measured and why it is not worth chasing.
       That element exists only in a 2.6 with __STEM__, so on 2.5.6 the parser
       does not know it, logs one "Invalid node name in skin" and builds the row
       without it. That line in the log is the only trace this file leaves on
       the running box, and it is a truthful one: the skin is carrying a widget
       the running Mixxx has never heard of.

       No TooltipId on any of these. The ids the stock skins use for stem
       widgets do not exist in 2.5.6 either, and an unknown one is a second
       warning for no gain. -->
  <SetVariable name="stemgroup">[Channel<Variable name="channel"/>_Stem<Variable name="stem"/>]</SetVariable>
  <SetVariable name="stemfx">[QuickEffectRack1_<Variable name="stemgroup"/>]</SetVariable>

  <WidgetGroup>
    <ObjectName>StemRow</ObjectName>
    <Layout>horizontal</Layout>
    <Size>0me,0me</Size>
    <Children>
      <StemLabel>
        <ObjectName>StemName</ObjectName>
        <Group>[Channel<Variable name="channel"/>]</Group>
        <StemNum><Variable name="stem"/></StemNum>
        <Alignment>left</Alignment>
        <Elide>right</Elide>
        <Size>66f,18f</Size>
        <Text></Text>
      </StemLabel>
      <PushButton>
        <ObjectName>StemMute</ObjectName>
        <Size>44f,22f</Size>
        <NumberStates>2</NumberStates>
        <State><Number>0</Number><Text>MUTE</Text></State>
        <State><Number>1</Number><Text>MUTE</Text></State>
        <!-- Lit means muted, which is what the word on it says. The stock
             skins invert theirs and light the button while the stem plays,
             but theirs carries no text to contradict. The control is a
             PowerWindow push button in the engine, so a tap latches and a
             held press is momentary; the widget only has to send the press
             and the release. -->
        <Connection>
          <ConfigKey><Variable name="stemgroup"/>,mute</ConfigKey>
          <ButtonState>LeftButton</ButtonState>
        </Connection>
      </PushButton>
      <!-- Both sliders are drawn from colours rather than from pixmaps:
           BarColor with BarWidth is a complete slider on its own, which is
           how a skin with one image file in it gets a fader. The handle is
           the one exception, a hairline the aspect ratio keeps thin, and it
           is here because a slider with no handle pixmap logs a line per
           instance, thirty-two of them across four decks. -->
      <SliderComposed>
        <ObjectName>StemVolume</ObjectName>
        <Size>0me,14f</Size>
        <Handle scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">images/stem_handle.svg</Handle>
        <Horizontal>true</Horizontal>
        <BarColor>#e5e6ea</BarColor>
        <BarWidth>6</BarWidth>
        <BarBgColor>#32323c</BarBgColor>
        <BarBgWidth>6</BarBgWidth>
        <BarMargins>3,3</BarMargins>
        <BarAxisPos>7</BarAxisPos>
        <BarRoundCaps>true</BarRoundCaps>
        <Connection>
          <ConfigKey><Variable name="stemgroup"/>,volume</ConfigKey>
        </Connection>
      </SliderComposed>
      <!-- The COLOR knob's stem: the super knob of the stem's own QuickEffect
           chain. Bipolar, because that knob rests in the middle and works
           both ways from there, so the bar is drawn from the centre out. -->
      <SliderComposed>
        <ObjectName>StemColor</ObjectName>
        <Size>0me,14f</Size>
        <Handle scalemode="STRETCH_ASPECT">images/stem_handle.svg</Handle>
        <Horizontal>true</Horizontal>
        <BarColor>#2d85cd</BarColor>
        <BarWidth>6</BarWidth>
        <BarBgColor>#32323c</BarBgColor>
        <BarBgWidth>6</BarBgWidth>
        <BarMargins>3,3</BarMargins>
        <BarAxisPos>7</BarAxisPos>
        <BarUnipolar>false</BarUnipolar>
        <BarRoundCaps>true</BarRoundCaps>
        <Connection>
          <ConfigKey><Variable name="stemfx"/>,super1</ConfigKey>
        </Connection>
      </SliderComposed>
    </Children>
  </WidgetGroup>
</Template>
